.TH PS 1
.SH NAME
ps \- process status
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ps
[
.B alx
]
[ pid ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ps
prints certain indicia about active
processes.
The
.B a
option asks for information about all processes with terminals (ordinarily
only one's own processes are displayed);
.B x
asks even about processes with no terminal;
.B l
asks for a long listing.
A numeric argument selects that one process and suppresses the short heading.
The short listing contains the process ID, tty letter,
the cumulative execution time of the process and the command name.
.PP
The long listing is columnar and contains
.TP
F
Flags associated with the process.
01: in core;
02: system process;
04: locked in core (e.g. for physical I/O);
010: being swapped;
020: being traced by another process.
.TP
S
The state of the process.
0: nonexistent;
S: sleeping;
W: waiting;
R: running;
I: intermediate;
Z: terminated;
T: stopped.
.TP
UID
The user ID of the process owner.
.TP
PID
The process ID of the process; as in certain cults it is possible to kill a process
if you know its true name.
.TP
PPID
The process ID of the parent process.
.TP
CPU
Processor utilization for scheduling.
.TP
PRI
The priority of the
process; high numbers mean low priority.
.TP
NICE
Used in priority computation.
This is the absolute value, not the increment: 20
.RB ( NZERO )
is normal and
.B nice \-10
makes it 30.
.TP
ADDR
The core address of the process if resident,
otherwise its block on the paging store.
.B Note:
this is a
.B word
address and it is printed in
.BR octal ,
where v7 printed a 64-byte click address in hex.
It is the number
.IR pstat (1M)'s
.B \-u
takes.
.TP
SZ
The size of the core image of the process, in
.BR words ,
a multiple of
.BR PGSZ .
.B Note:
v7 counted 64-byte blocks.
.TP
WCHAN
The event for which the process is waiting or sleeping;
if blank, the process is running.
.B Note:
octal, and the whole of it \- v7 masked it to 24 bits.
.TP
TTY
The controlling tty for the process, as an index into the kernel's
one terminal array, or `?' if there is none.
.TP
TIME
The cumulative execution time for the process.
.B Note:
this is
.B u_utime + u_stime
in ticks at
.BR HZ ,
which is 250 here and was 60 on the PDP-11.
.TP
CMD
The command name.
.DT
.PP
A process that has exited and has a parent, but has not
yet been waited for by the parent is marked <defunct>.
Its core image has already been given back, so nothing but its
.B proc
entry is printed.
.PP
A process that has been swapped out is marked <swapped>, and has no
TTY, TIME or CMD.
Its u-area is on the paging store; v7 read it back off
.I /dev/swap
and this
.I ps
does not.
.PP
.B Note:
.B the command name is exact.
v7 made "an educated guess as to the file name and arguments given when the
process was created by examining core memory or the swap area", warned that
the method was unreliable, and needed some 140 lines to do it.
On this system the name is
.B u_comm
in the u-area, put there by
.IR exec (2),
so there is nothing to guess \- but there are also no
.BR arguments ,
only the name, and it is at most
.B DIRSIZ
characters.
.SH FILES
None.
Everything
.I ps
prints comes through two
.IR kctl (2)
calls \-
.B KCTL_GET
of the
.B proc
table, and
.B KCTL_PSINFO
for the four u-area fields per slot \- and
.B neither is privileged,
so this is an ordinary user's command.
.PP
.B Note:
it used to read
.I /dev/kmem
and
.I /dev/mem
for those four fields, and since both are mode 0640 and root's it was the
super-user's program.
Those modes have not changed and
.I ps
is deliberately not set-user-ID; what changed is that it no longer asks
for memory.
A set-uid
.I ps
would hand out every process's memory through a program that already knows
the layout, which is why
.B KCTL_PSINFO
returns a digest and not an address.
.PP
.B Note:
v7 also read
.IR /unix ,
.I /dev/swap
and the
.I /dev
directory, and had a
.B k
option naming
.I /usr/sys/core
and an optional
.I namelist
argument.
There is no kernel image on this filesystem, so all of that is gone;
.IR kctl (2)
is what replaced it.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
kctl(2), pstat(1M), iostat(1M), nice(1), kill(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 if any process was printed and 1 if none was, which is
what makes
.B ps
.I pid
usable as a test.
.SH BUGS
Things can change while
.I ps
is running; the picture it gives is only a close
approximation to reality.
.br
The u-area of a resident process other than the caller's is as of that
process's last context switch, that being when the kernel last flushed it
to the process's own image.
The caller's own row is current, because the live copy at
.B UBASE
is used instead whenever it is the one that belongs to the process being
printed.
That rule did not go away when the reading moved into the kernel; it is
now
.IR kernel/kctl.c 's.
.br
Some data printed for defunct processes is irrelevant.
